{
  "analysis": "Let's read the problem statement very carefully:\n\"Inputs (read-only) under `/task/input`:\n- `trio.vcf.gz` (+ `.tbi`) - the provider's joint call set of the three family members, chromosome 22 only, FORMAT fields `GT:AD:DP:GQ`. The provider states that the file is complete with respect to the reference sequence the provider worked with: at every position of that reference which is not covered by a record of the file, all three family members are homozygous for the base of the provider's reference.\n...\n- `resources/GRCh37.chr22.fa.gz`, `resources/GRCh38.chr22.fa.gz` (+ `.fai`, `.gzi`) - chromosome 22 of both human assemblies, bgzip-compressed, exactly as they are in use at the provider and at the laboratory (sequence outside the regions the laboratory works on is masked with N; coordinates are those of the full chromosome)\n- `resources/hg19ToHg38.chr22.over.chain`, `resources/hg38ToHg19.chr22.over.chain` - UCSC liftOver chain files restricted to chromosome 22\n\nYour task:\n1. Deliver the call set of the family on GRCh38 in the laboratory's canonical representation.\n2. Report which input alleles lie in sequence that cannot be placed on GRCh38.\n3. Apply the laboratory SOP and report the candidate causal variants of the affected child.\n\nRequired outputs under `/task/output`:\n- `trio.GRCh38.vcf.gz` plus its index (`.tbi` or `.csi`) - bgzip-compressed VCF on GRCh38 with the contig named `chr22`, sorted by position. It describes how the three family members differ from the GRCh38 reference sequence: it holds every allele that differs from the GRCh38 reference and is carried by at least one family member, for all GRCh38 positions that the provided chain file aligns to the provider's reference (the aligned blocks of the chain; sequence that the chain does not align and N-masked sequence are out of scope), and nothing else. One ALT allele per record; every record in left-aligned, minimal (parsimonious) representation with `REF` equal to the GRCh38 reference sequence; differences of single bases are written as single-base records. The same three samples under their original names. `GT` of a sample states how many of its two chromosome copies carry the ALT allele of that record (`0/0`, `0/1` or `1/1`); a copy that carries any other allele counts as `0` in that record.\n\n- `unmapped.tsv` - tab-separated with header `chrom_in`, `pos_in`, `ref`, `alt`: one row per ALT allele of the input that lies at a position of the provider's reference which the provided chain file does not align to GRCh38 chromosome 22, with chromosome, position and alleles as written in the input file. An input allele at an aligned position is never listed here, whatever it turns out to be on GRCh38. Header only if there is none.\n\n- `candidates.tsv` - tab-separated with header `chrom`, `pos`, `ref`, `alt`, `gene`, `consequence`, `model`: one row per candidate variant under the SOP. `chrom`, `pos`, `ref`, `alt` exactly as in your `trio.GRCh38.vcf.gz`; `gene` is the gene symbol as written in `panel_genes.txt`; `consequence` is the qualifying class that the SOP assigns to the variant; `model` is one of `de_novo`, `compound_het`, `homozygous_recessive`. Header only if there is no candidate.\"\n\nLet's unpack every single detail!",
  "plan": "Write a python script to inspect the chain file, the aligned blocks, the masking in GRCh38, the input VCF records, and check which chain file is 'the provided chain file'. Notice there are two chain files: hg19ToHg38.chr22.over.chain and hg38ToHg19.chr22.over.chain.",
